2|0@Machinists^1440875974#cujzckc:RyanGBaker$:Unless you were a career KKK event organizer or something, there's almost no job that could possibly hurt to have on a resume. I think you will find that temp jobs especially, even if just operating a band saw or something menial, are not only an asset to you in terms of job experience, but a welcome feather in your cap for future job applications. It tells the employer "yeah, I've worked in a shop before and I have an idea of what's expected of me." If they did not state that the training was unpaid, it's likely that they will be paying you at least a probationary wage (probably $10-12/hr) during your training, with the full wage upon hire. If you can get past the idea of working 3rd shift (I work 12 hr night shifts and it's not that bad), you should definitely be taking the opportunity. If you can't, you'll probably want to pass, because it sounds like that's going to be your only in for the position. Now, the only thing left to address is how guaranteed the job is. The only insight I can offer is that if the company is reaching out for people to fill the position, this both helps and hurts your cause. It helps because they definitely need someone in that position, but it also hurts (especially with a 'candidate' system) because you're not the only candidate and you must prove yourself. If you think that only a few people are bidding on the position, then this should be no issue.
