1|0@Machinists^1440885999#cuk4lcy:La_Guy_Person$:Its hard to say because they can very quite a bit. I get a 15% differential with a $3 cap (basically, once you hit a base pay of $20 you hit the $3 cap). Our weekend shifts get 20% but with the three dollar cap, you'll never see the extra 5% if your at all experienced. I know of shops in my area that have higher differentials and shops in my area that dont have caps. I also have friends (not machinists) that get as little as $.50 differentials for working off shifts in factorys. So it can very a lot and a poor differential might be out weighed by a good base pay or good retirement plan or other benefits. It all depends on what you're looking for. I could easily make more money elsewhere but I like the type of work I'm doing and the amount of experience I'm getting right now. Edit:whats your dif? If you don't mind me asking. 
