1|0@Machinists^1440993980#culhqr2:RyanGBaker$:G12/G13 aren't even canned cycles... Also, G91 is only needed for G12/G13 if you're doing incremental cuts with loop counts (designated with an L value). For example, once you have your cutter positioned at the center of the hole and at Z0, you could use G91 on the line that activates the pocket milling cycle with a Z value of -.250 and an L value of 4 to make 4 cuts progressing .250" in depth each pass, for a final depth of 1.000". If you are using one depth, G91 is not required as you can simply use the absolute depth that you want to go to.
1|0@Machinists^1440994447#culhyii:EdwinNJ$:I get how and when to use that G91 My question was why the hell is it needed. Every literature I've read said it IS a kind of canned cycle. It may be handled on a more fundamental level, but it's still a kind of canned cycle, or, again, so everything I've read says. But doesn't requiring the end user to add in your parameters negate the concept of encapsulation? Like, why have the functionality of being able to make/have canned cycles if you're going to make the user just enter in the stuff you were supposed to. In computer programming, they teach you to neatly handle all this stuff when writing your own subroutines. I know CNC programming isn't the same, but in some ways they're similar, for example, in writing canned cycles, which are essentially a kind of subroutine. Does EVERY canned cycle need that G91 if there's going to be an L-word for multiple iterations, or is the G12/13 a special exception for some reason? I know there are CNC ninjas who'd understand this issue EXACTLY, for one, my CNC teacher knows a guy who worked at Cimquest and also did post-processing work who'd know, so if you don't know the exact details, it's fine. This might involve really fundamental understanding of the machine at the machine level. But that's what I'm trying to find out, is this a special excpetion, and if so why? And if not, then why couldn't they have gotten rid of the G91 requirement when they designed these machines?
1|0@Machinists^1440997005#culj2g5:RyanGBaker$:From what I understand, there's no option to use words for incremental depth due to control limitations. If I remember correctly, Haas controls can only handle up to 9 commands/words per line. There are already existing words for the command for making multiple passes of incremental diameter. If you use every available word for the command (including the L word), as well as the command itself and G91, you have 9 total commands and words. Why G91 and L can't be replaced with words representing starting depth and incremental depth is somewhat beyond me. It could be that doing that would do away with loop counts on the cycles, which would make multiple passes at the same depth and diameter impossible. That has limited use, but use nonetheless. It could also be due to limitations that would cause incremental step-overs and incremental depths to conflict, possibly when determining which would incremental movement would take priority. Finally, G7x/G8x canned cycles DO have the same quirk. You have to specify G91 with a loop count to do multiple-depth cycles with them (with exception to pecking cycles, of course). However, with canned cycles, it will perform the operation at each increment at every coordinate before going down to the next increment and going through every coordinate again (again, with exception to pecking cycles).
1|0@Machinists^1440997565#culjani:EdwinNJ$:"Why G91 and L can't be replaced with words representing starting depth and incremental depth" No, not even that. How about if it just didn't need the G91? Just an L word for number of iterations of doing the cycle. That is, the original programmers or whatever use the G91 in THEIR code, and the end user doesn't have to bother with it? We do that shit all the time with traditional programming. If you make the end user of your subroutine have to enter in weird technical stuff, you wrote a crappy subroutine (again, this is in computer programming). Whatever the reason is, it's something really technical. And stuff like this is a horrible brain worm for me; I HAVE to find out what the reason is. I have half a mind to call my CNC instructor and get this post-processor guy's name/info.
